{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.75,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J12", "U5"],
      "pins": ["J12.A4", "U5.1", "U5.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VBUS"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the USB-C receptacle J12 pin A4 (the VBUS pin) is attached to BOTH the +5V global label and the VBUS global label. On the Power sheet U5 (TPS2121RUXR) pin 1 OUT is on the VBUS net and pin 2 IN2 is on the +5V net. Because J12.A4 carries both labels, the +5V net and the VBUS net are merged into one net, so the mux's IN2 is shorted directly to its OUT, and the USB connector's VBUS pin is hard-wired to the mux output rail.",
      "expected": "Per the specification the USB VBUS pin must feed the mux input IN2 on the +5V net, and the mux output must be a separate VBUS net: 'J12 ... VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) -> +5V net -> U5 pin 2/2_1 (IN2)' and 'note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux.' J12.A4 should carry only the +5V label, never VBUS.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "J12 (USB_C_Receptacle_USB2.0_14P) VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) -> +5V net -> U5 pin 2/2_1 (IN2)."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch (page 1/4), top-right: J12 pin A4 wire carries a +5V global label and a VBUS global label; power.kicad_sch (page 4/4): U5 pin 1 OUT = VBUS, U5 pin 2 IN2 = +5V."}
      ],
      "impact": "The TPS2121RUXR power mux is defeated: its IN2 and OUT are the same node, so it cannot perform seamless switchover between the 12 V barrel jack and USB. When the 12 V supply is connected, the mux output (now = VBUS = USB pin A4) drives 12 V onto the USB-C VBUS pin, back-powering the host port and potentially damaging it; the +5V/IN2 input also loses its independent source.",
      "confidence": 0.75
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic contains a net-merge anomaly: the USB-C VBUS pin (J12.A4) is labeled with both +5V and VBUS, tying the mux input IN2 to the mux output OUT and hard-wiring the USB connector to the mux output rail, which contradicts the spec's requirement that +5V (USB) and VBUS (mux output) be distinct nets and bypasses the power-mux arbitration."
}

